{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAs behooves any intelligent schoolboy, Kevin Sun is studying psycowlogy, cowculus, and cryptcowgraphy at the Bovinia State University (BGU) under Farmer Ivan. During his Mathematics of Olympiads (MoO) class, Kevin was confronted with a weird functional equation and needs your help. For two fixed integers k and p, where p is an odd prime number, the functional equation states that  for some function . (This equation should hold for any integer x in the range 0 to p\u2009-\u20091, inclusive.)It turns out that f can actually be many different functions. Instead of finding a solution, Kevin wants you to count the number of distinct functions f that satisfy this equation. Since the answer may be very large, you should print your result modulo 109\u2009+\u20097.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++ code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nusing ll = long long;\nusing ull = unsigned long long;\nusing ld = long double;\nusing pii = pair<int, int>;\nusing vi = vector<int>;\nusing vii = vector<pii>;\nusing pll = pair<ll, ll>;\n\nconst int MOD = 1000000007;\nconst int INF_INT = 1000000000;\nconst ll INF_LL = 1000000000000000000LL;\nconst double PI = 2 * acos(0.0);\nconst int MAX_M = 50;\nconst int MAX_N = 1000007;\n\nll modPow(ll base, ll exp, ll mod) {\n    ll result = 1;\n    base %= mod;\n    while (exp > 0) {\n        if (exp & 1) result = (result * base) % mod;\n        base = (base * base) % mod;\n        exp >>= 1;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n\nll modPow(ll base, ll exp) {\n    return modPow(base, exp, MOD);\n}\n\nll modPowCustom(ll base, ll exp, ll mod) {\n    if (exp == 0) return 1 % mod;\n    if (exp & 1) return (base * modPowCustom(base, exp - 1, mod)) % mod;\n    ll half = modPowCustom(base, exp >> 1, mod);\n    return (half * half) % mod;\n}\n\nint n, k;\nint a[MAX_N];\nbool visited[MAX_N];\n\nvoid solve() {\n    cin >> n >> k;\n    \n    if (k == 0) {\n        cout << modPow(k, k - 1);\n        return;\n    }\n    if (k == 1) {\n        cout << modPow(k, k);\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    for (int d = 0; d < n; ++d) {\n        a[d] = (ll)d * k % n;\n    }\n    \n    ll ans = 1;\n    for (int d = 0; d < n; ++d) {\n        if (!visited[d]) {\n            visited[d] = true;\n            int current = a[d];\n            int cycleLength = 1;\n            while (!visited[current]) {\n                visited[current] = true;\n                current = a[current];\n                ++cycleLength;\n            }\n            if (modPowCustom(k, cycleLength, n) == 1) {\n                ans = (ans * n) % MOD;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    cout << ans;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n#ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE\n    freopen(\"in.txt\", \"r\", stdin);\n#endif\n\n    solve();\n    \n#ifdef _LOCAL_\n    // cerr << \"Time: \" << (double)(clock()) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC << \"s\\n\";\n#endif\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nusing ll = long long;\nusing pii = pair<int, int>;\nusing vi = vector<int>;\n\nconst int MOD = 1000000007;\n\nll modPow(ll base, ll exp, ll mod) {\n    ll result = 1;\n    base %= mod;\n    while (exp > 0) {\n        if (exp & 1) result = (result * base) % mod;\n        base = (base * base) % mod;\n        exp >>= 1;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n    int n, k;\n    cin >> n >> k;\n    \n    // The functional equation is f(k*x mod p) = f(x)\n    // We need to count functions f: {0,1,...,p-1} -> {0,1,...,p-1} satisfying this\n    // The number of such functions is n^(number of cycles in the mapping x -> k*x mod n)\n    \n    // Special case: if n=1, there's only one element\n    if (n == 1) {\n        cout << 1 << endl;\n        return 0;\n    }\n    \n    // Count cycles in the functional graph of x -> (k*x) mod n\n    vi visited(n, 0);\n    int cycleCount = 0;\n    \n    for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {\n        if (visited[start]) continue;\n        \n        int current = start;\n        while (!visited[current]) {\n            visited[current] = 1;\n            current = (1LL * k * current) % n;\n        }\n        cycleCount++;\n    }\n    \n    // Number of functions is n^(cycleCount) mod MOD\n    ll result = modPow(n, cycleCount, MOD);\n    cout << result << endl;\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.7\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-3.933898824470816e-06, -0.00022516099852509797, -2.0265558760002023e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -4.279521817807108e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.0874729418428615e-05, -1.156323378381785e-05, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -8.106198947643861e-06, -9.536738616588991e-07, -2.634490556374658e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.008245829492807388, -0.0001394651480950415, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -7.1